insert into kairo.questions (id, subject, topic, subtopic, learning_objective, concepts_tested, difficulty_rating, cognitive_level, estimated_solving_time_sec, reading_load, calculation_load, distractors, source, year, exam_body, stem, options, correct_option, explanation, lifecycle_state, distractor_rationale) values
($q$chemistry_0422$q$, $q$Chemistry$q$, $q$Acids, Bases & Salts$q$, $q$pH Scale$q$, $q$Answer a real JAMB 2005 question on pH Scale.$q$, $q$[{"conceptId": "ab1gw8", "weight": 1}]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$application$q$, 45, $q$low$q$, $q$light$q$, $q$[{"option": "A", "explanation": "Ethanoic acid is a weak acid, so its pH will be below 7. We are looking for a basic solution to get a high pH.", "misconceptionId": "confused_similar_concepts"}, {"option": "C", "explanation": "Hydrochloric acid is a very strong acid, which means it will have a very LOW pH (close to 0 or 1).", "misconceptionId": "misunderstood_terminology"}, {"option": "D", "explanation": "You correctly identified this as a base, but it is a weak base. In a contest for the 'highest pH,' the strong base always wins over the weak one.", "misconceptionId": "memorized_not_understood"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$jamb_past_question$q$, 2005, $q$JAMB$q$, $q$Which of the following equimolar solutions will have the highest pH?$q$, $q$[{"label": "A", "text": "CH3COOH", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "B", "text": "NaOH", "isCorrect": true}, {"label": "C", "text": "HCl", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "D", "text": "NH4OH", "isCorrect": false}]$q$::jsonb, $q$B$q$, $q$To solve this, you need to remember that the pH scale runs from 0 to 14. A 'high pH' means the solution is very basic. Sodium Hydroxide (NaOH) is a strong base, meaning it dissociates completely in water to release a maximum amount of hydroxide ions (OH-). While NH4OH is also a base, it's a weak one, so it won't push the pH as high as NaOH will for the same concentration.$q$, $q$live$q$, $q$A: Ethanoic acid is a weak acid, so its pH will be below 7. We are looking for a basic solution to get a high pH. C: Hydrochloric acid is a very strong acid, which means it will have a very LOW pH (close to 0 or 1). D: You correctly identified this as a base, but it is a weak base. In a contest for the 'highest pH,' the strong base always wins over the weak one.$q$),
($q$chemistry_0423$q$, $q$Chemistry$q$, $q$Electrolysis$q$, $q$Faraday's Laws$q$, $q$Answer a real JAMB 2005 question on Faraday's Laws.$q$, $q$[{"conceptId": "cbvosp", "weight": 1}]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$application$q$, 57, $q$low$q$, $q$light$q$, $q$[{"option": "A", "explanation": "Don't let the '4.0' fool you into thinking it's the largest. Since Chloride has a valency of 1, it only needs 4 Faradays total.", "misconceptionId": "rushed_under_pressure"}, {"option": "B", "explanation": "This requires the least amount of electricity (3F) because both the mole count and the charge are relatively low.", "misconceptionId": "guessed_no_reasoning"}, {"option": "C", "explanation": "Copper has a valency of 2, so 2.5x2=5F. This is a high amount, but Aluminum's valency of 3 still requires more.", "misconceptionId": "arithmetic_slip"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$jamb_past_question$q$, 2005, $q$JAMB$q$, $q$Which of the following ions require the largest quantity of electricity for discharge at an electrode?$q$, $q$[{"label": "A", "text": "4.0 moles of Cl-", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "B", "text": "3.0 moles of Na+", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "C", "text": "2.5 moles of Cu2+", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "D", "text": "2.0 moles of Al3+", "isCorrect": true}]$q$::jsonb, $q$D$q$, $q$The quantity of electricity (Q) depends on both the number of moles and the charge (valency) of the ion: Q = n x z x F. A (4x1=4F), B (3x1=3F), C (2.5x2=5F), D (2.0x3=6F). Even though Aluminum has the fewest moles, its +3 charge means it needs more electricity per atom to discharge, making it the winner with 6 Faradays.$q$, $q$live$q$, $q$A: Don't let the '4.0' fool you into thinking it's the largest. Since Chloride has a valency of 1, it only needs 4 Faradays total. B: This requires the least amount of electricity (3F) because both the mole count and the charge are relatively low. C: Copper has a valency of 2, so 2.5x2=5F. This is a high amount, but Aluminum's valency of 3 still requires more.$q$),
($q$chemistry_0424$q$, $q$Chemistry$q$, $q$Acids, Bases & Salts$q$, $q$Acid-Base Indicators$q$, $q$Answer a real JAMB 2005 question on Acid-Base Indicators.$q$, $q$[{"conceptId": "zjw5u3", "weight": 1}]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$recall$q$, 25, $q$low$q$, $q$none$q$, $q$[{"option": "A", "explanation": "Phenolphthalein turns red/pink in bases, not acids. You might be confusing it with Litmus paper.", "misconceptionId": "confused_similar_concepts"}, {"option": "B", "explanation": "Orange is the colour of Methyl Orange in neutral or slightly basic environments, not phenolphthalein.", "misconceptionId": "memorized_not_understood"}, {"option": "D", "explanation": "Yellow is typically the colour of Methyl Orange in basic solutions or Bromothymol Blue in acids.", "misconceptionId": "confused_similar_concepts"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$jamb_past_question$q$, 2005, $q$JAMB$q$, $q$Phenolphthalein in acidic solution is$q$, $q$[{"label": "A", "text": "red", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "B", "text": "orange", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "C", "text": "colourless", "isCorrect": true}, {"label": "D", "text": "yellow", "isCorrect": false}]$q$::jsonb, $q$C$q$, $q$Indicators are substances that change colour depending on the acidity of a solution. Phenolphthalein is a very common indicator that stays completely clear (colourless) in acids but turns a bright pink or red in basic (alkaline) solutions.$q$, $q$live$q$, $q$A: Phenolphthalein turns red/pink in bases, not acids. You might be confusing it with Litmus paper. B: Orange is the colour of Methyl Orange in neutral or slightly basic environments, not phenolphthalein. D: Yellow is typically the colour of Methyl Orange in basic solutions or Bromothymol Blue in acids.$q$),
($q$chemistry_0425$q$, $q$Chemistry$q$, $q$Chemical Equilibrium$q$, $q$Thermodynamics of Equilibrium$q$, $q$Answer a real JAMB 2005 question on Thermodynamics of Equilibrium.$q$, $q$[{"conceptId": "pns65n", "weight": 1}]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$application$q$, 57, $q$low$q$, $q$light$q$, $q$[{"option": "A", "explanation": "'Neutral' isn't a standard term for describing Gibbs Free Energy values in thermodynamics.", "misconceptionId": "misunderstood_terminology"}, {"option": "B", "explanation": "If \u0394G is zero, the system is at equilibrium. Rusting iron is moving forward, not staying in a balanced state.", "misconceptionId": "memorized_not_understood"}, {"option": "C", "explanation": "A positive \u0394G means the reaction is non-spontaneous, needing constant energy added to occur. Rusting happens on its own!", "misconceptionId": "confused_similar_concepts"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$jamb_past_question$q$, 2005, $q$JAMB$q$, $q$When iron is exposed to moisture and it rusts, the value of ΔG for the reaction is$q$, $q$[{"label": "A", "text": "neutral", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "B", "text": "zero", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "C", "text": "positive", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "D", "text": "negative", "isCorrect": true}]$q$::jsonb, $q$D$q$, $q$The trick here is to identify if the process is spontaneous. Since iron rusts by itself when left in moisture, it is spontaneous. For any spontaneous process, the Gibbs Free Energy change (ΔG) must be negative.$q$, $q$live$q$, $q$A: 'Neutral' isn't a standard term for describing Gibbs Free Energy values in thermodynamics. B: If ΔG is zero, the system is at equilibrium. Rusting iron is moving forward, not staying in a balanced state. C: A positive ΔG means the reaction is non-spontaneous, needing constant energy added to occur. Rusting happens on its own!$q$),
($q$chemistry_0426$q$, $q$Chemistry$q$, $q$Acids, Bases & Salts$q$, $q$IUPAC Nomenclature of Inorganic Ions$q$, $q$Answer a real JAMB 2005 question on IUPAC Nomenclature of Inorganic Ions.$q$, $q$[{"conceptId": "zmdoiy", "weight": 1}]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$application$q$, 57, $q$low$q$, $q$light$q$, $q$[{"option": "A", "explanation": "You got the 'dioxo' part right, but your oxidation number calculation resulted in 4 instead of 3.", "misconceptionId": "formula_recall_error"}, {"option": "B", "explanation": "Trioxonitrate refers to an ion with 3 oxygen atoms (like NO3-), but this ion only has 2.", "misconceptionId": "misunderstood_terminology"}, {"option": "C", "explanation": "This option incorrectly combines the wrong oxygen count (Tri) with the wrong oxidation number (IV).", "misconceptionId": "guessed_no_reasoning"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$jamb_past_question$q$, 2005, $q$JAMB$q$, $q$What is the correct IUPAC name for NO2-?$q$, $q$[{"label": "A", "text": "Dioxonitrate (IV) ion", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "B", "text": "Trioxonitrate (III) ion", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "C", "text": "Trioxonitrate (IV) ion", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "D", "text": "Dioxonitrate (III) ion", "isCorrect": true}]$q$::jsonb, $q$D$q$, $q$Number of oxygens: 2 means dioxo. Oxidation state of N: x+2(-2)=-1, so x=+3. Name: Dioxonitrate (III). Nitrate (V) would be NO3-.$q$, $q$live$q$, $q$A: You got the 'dioxo' part right, but your oxidation number calculation resulted in 4 instead of 3. B: Trioxonitrate refers to an ion with 3 oxygen atoms (like NO3-), but this ion only has 2. C: This option incorrectly combines the wrong oxygen count (Tri) with the wrong oxidation number (IV).$q$),
($q$chemistry_0427$q$, $q$Chemistry$q$, $q$Chemical Combination$q$, $q$Mass-Mass Stoichiometry$q$, $q$Answer a real JAMB 2005 question on Mass-Mass Stoichiometry.$q$, $q$[{"conceptId": "e5q9c8", "weight": 1}]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$application$q$, 72, $q$low$q$, $q$heavy$q$, $q$[{"option": "A", "explanation": "This might happen if you mistakenly thought the reaction ratio was 2:1 or made an error in calculating the molar mass.", "misconceptionId": "arithmetic_slip"}, {"option": "C", "explanation": "This looks like a number obtained by multiplying 5.6 by some factor instead of dividing by the molar mass.", "misconceptionId": "formula_recall_error"}, {"option": "D", "explanation": "You might have simply moved the decimal point on the mass (5.6) without dividing by the molar mass (56).", "misconceptionId": "rushed_under_pressure"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$jamb_past_question$q$, 2005, $q$JAMB$q$, $q$How many moles of limestone (CaCO3) will be required to produce 5.6g of CaO? [Ca = 40, C = 12, O= 16]$q$, $q$[{"label": "A", "text": "0.20 mol", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "B", "text": "0.10 mol", "isCorrect": true}, {"label": "C", "text": "1.12 mol", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "D", "text": "0.56 mol", "isCorrect": false}]$q$::jsonb, $q$B$q$, $q$CaCO3 -> CaO + CO2, a 1:1 ratio. Molar mass of CaO = 40+16=56g/mol. 5.6/56 = 0.1 moles CaO, so 0.1 moles CaCO3 needed.$q$, $q$live$q$, $q$A: This might happen if you mistakenly thought the reaction ratio was 2:1 or made an error in calculating the molar mass. C: This looks like a number obtained by multiplying 5.6 by some factor instead of dividing by the molar mass. D: You might have simply moved the decimal point on the mass (5.6) without dividing by the molar mass (56).$q$),
($q$chemistry_0428$q$, $q$Chemistry$q$, $q$Separation of Mixtures$q$, $q$Separation Techniques$q$, $q$Answer a real JAMB 2005 question on Separation Techniques.$q$, $q$[{"conceptId": "kc1jua", "weight": 1}]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$application$q$, 57, $q$low$q$, $q$light$q$, $q$[{"option": "A", "explanation": "Diffusion is used to separate gases on a small scale in a lab (Graham's Law), but it's not the primary method for bulk industrial production of N2 and O2.", "misconceptionId": "confused_similar_concepts"}, {"option": "B", "explanation": "While they have different densities, this is not a practical or efficient way to separate a mixture of gases into pure components.", "misconceptionId": "guessed_no_reasoning"}, {"option": "D", "explanation": "Solubility is used to remove certain impurities (like scrubbing CO2), but not to separate the main components of air.", "misconceptionId": "memorized_not_understood"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$jamb_past_question$q$, 2005, $q$JAMB$q$, $q$The property used in the industrial preparation of nitrogen and oxygen from air is$q$, $q$[{"label": "A", "text": "rate of diffusion", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "B", "text": "density", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "C", "text": "boiling point", "isCorrect": true}, {"label": "D", "text": "solubility", "isCorrect": false}]$q$::jsonb, $q$C$q$, $q$Air is liquefied and undergoes fractional distillation. Nitrogen boils at -196C and Oxygen at -183C, allowing separate collection. Fractional distillation = boiling point.$q$, $q$live$q$, $q$A: Diffusion is used to separate gases on a small scale in a lab (Graham's Law), but it's not the primary method for bulk industrial production of N2 and O2. B: While they have different densities, this is not a practical or efficient way to separate a mixture of gases into pure components. D: Solubility is used to remove certain impurities (like scrubbing CO2), but not to separate the main components of air.$q$),
($q$chemistry_0429$q$, $q$Chemistry$q$, $q$Metals & Their Compounds$q$, $q$Alloys$q$, $q$Answer a real JAMB 2005 question on Alloys.$q$, $q$[{"conceptId": "4ihrpp", "weight": 1}]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$recall$q$, 37, $q$low$q$, $q$none$q$, $q$[{"option": "A", "explanation": "You correctly spotted Steel, but Duralumin is an Aluminum alloy (Al, Cu, Mg, Mn); it doesn't contain iron.", "misconceptionId": "confused_similar_concepts"}, {"option": "B", "explanation": "Brass (Cu+Zn) and Bronze (Cu+Sn) are copper alloys. No iron here!", "misconceptionId": "memorized_not_understood"}, {"option": "D", "explanation": "Solder is Lead and Tin, and Duralumin is Aluminum-based. Neither contains iron.", "misconceptionId": "guessed_no_reasoning"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$jamb_past_question$q$, 2005, $q$JAMB$q$, $q$Which of the following alloys contain iron?$q$, $q$[{"label": "A", "text": "Duralumin and steel", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "B", "text": "Brass and bronze", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "C", "text": "Steel and permalloy", "isCorrect": true}, {"label": "D", "text": "Soft solder and duralumin", "isCorrect": false}]$q$::jsonb, $q$C$q$, $q$Steel is an alloy of iron and carbon. Permalloy is a magnetic alloy of iron and nickel. Duralumin is Aluminum-based; Brass and Bronze are copper-based.$q$, $q$live$q$, $q$A: You correctly spotted Steel, but Duralumin is an Aluminum alloy (Al, Cu, Mg, Mn); it doesn't contain iron. B: Brass (Cu+Zn) and Bronze (Cu+Sn) are copper alloys. No iron here! D: Solder is Lead and Tin, and Duralumin is Aluminum-based. Neither contains iron.$q$),
($q$chemistry_0430$q$, $q$Chemistry$q$, $q$Metals & Their Compounds$q$, $q$Extraction of Iron — Impurities and Slag$q$, $q$Answer a real JAMB 2006 question on Extraction of Iron — Impurities and Slag.$q$, $q$[{"conceptId": "hse3ru", "weight": 1}]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$recall$q$, 25, $q$low$q$, $q$none$q$, $q$[{"option": "A", "explanation": "Calcium sulphate is not used in smelting; it wouldn't help remove the silica impurities effectively like limestone does.", "misconceptionId": "confused_similar_concepts"}, {"option": "C", "explanation": "Copper sulphate is a salt of a different metal entirely and has no place in the extraction of iron.", "misconceptionId": "guessed_no_reasoning"}, {"option": "D", "explanation": "Zinc nitrate is irrelevant to the metallurgy of iron; you might be confusing the flux with random inorganic salts.", "misconceptionId": "memorized_not_understood"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$jamb_past_question$q$, 2006, $q$JAMB$q$, $q$The raw materials used for the smelting of iron ore in a furnace is$q$, $q$[{"label": "A", "text": "CaSO4", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "B", "text": "CaCO3", "isCorrect": true}, {"label": "C", "text": "CuSO4", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "D", "text": "Zn(NO3)2", "isCorrect": false}]$q$::jsonb, $q$B$q$, $q$Blast furnace needs coke, hot air, and limestone (CaCO3). Limestone decomposes to CaO which reacts with silica impurities to form slag.$q$, $q$live$q$, $q$A: Calcium sulphate is not used in smelting; it wouldn't help remove the silica impurities effectively like limestone does. C: Copper sulphate is a salt of a different metal entirely and has no place in the extraction of iron. D: Zinc nitrate is irrelevant to the metallurgy of iron; you might be confusing the flux with random inorganic salts.$q$),
($q$chemistry_0431$q$, $q$Chemistry$q$, $q$Chemical Equilibrium$q$, $q$Thermodynamics of Equilibrium$q$, $q$Answer a real JAMB 2006 question on Thermodynamics of Equilibrium.$q$, $q$[{"conceptId": "pns65n", "weight": 1}]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$application$q$, 72, $q$medium$q$, $q$moderate$q$, $q$[{"option": "A", "explanation": "This is a common error where students see a negative entropy (\u0394S) and assume it automatically makes the reaction non-spontaneous, forgetting that a large negative enthalpy can outweigh it.", "misconceptionId": "overgeneralized_rule"}, {"option": "C", "explanation": "The reaction proceeds in the reverse direction only if \u0394G is positive. Our calculation shows it is negative.", "misconceptionId": "formula_recall_error"}, {"option": "D", "explanation": "While it is true it would be spontaneous at lower temperatures, the question asks what happens at the given temperature, where it is already spontaneous.", "misconceptionId": "rushed_under_pressure"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$jamb_past_question$q$, 2006, $q$JAMB$q$, $q$H2(g) + Br2(g) -> 2HBr(g). The above reaction is carried out at 25C. ΔH is -72 kJ mol-1 and ΔS is -106 J mol-1K-1. The reaction will?$q$, $q$[{"label": "A", "text": "not proceed spontaneously at the given temperature", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "B", "text": "proceed spontaneously at the given temperature", "isCorrect": true}, {"label": "C", "text": "proceed in the reverse direction at the given temperature", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "D", "text": "proceed spontaneously at lower temperature", "isCorrect": false}]$q$::jsonb, $q$B$q$, $q$ΔG = ΔH - TΔS (convert ΔS to kJ). ΔG = -72 - (298 x -0.106) = -72+31.588 = -40.412 kJ. Negative ΔG means spontaneous at this temperature.$q$, $q$live$q$, $q$A: This is a common error where students see a negative entropy (ΔS) and assume it automatically makes the reaction non-spontaneous, forgetting that a large negative enthalpy can outweigh it. C: The reaction proceeds in the reverse direction only if ΔG is positive. Our calculation shows it is negative. D: While it is true it would be spontaneous at lower temperatures, the question asks what happens at the given temperature, where it is already spontaneous.$q$),
($q$chemistry_0432$q$, $q$Chemistry$q$, $q$Chemical Combination$q$, $q$Vapour Density and Molar Mass$q$, $q$Answer a real JAMB 2006 question on Vapour Density and Molar Mass.$q$, $q$[{"conceptId": "m4b114", "weight": 1}]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$application$q$, 72, $q$low$q$, $q$moderate$q$, $q$[{"option": "A", "explanation": "This is Propane (C3H8). Its molar mass would be 44, which doesn't match our calculated 40.", "misconceptionId": "arithmetic_slip"}, {"option": "B", "explanation": "This is Propene (C3H6). Its molar mass is 42, which is close but not the 40 we need.", "misconceptionId": "confused_similar_concepts"}, {"option": "D", "explanation": "This is Ethene (C2H4). It only has 24g of carbon per mole, not 36g.", "misconceptionId": "missing_prerequisite"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$jamb_past_question$q$, 2006, $q$JAMB$q$, $q$One mole of a hydrocarbon contains 36g of carbon and its vapour density is 20. The structure of the hydrocarbon is$q$, $q$[{"label": "A", "text": "CH3CH2CH3", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "B", "text": "CH3CH=CH2", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "C", "text": "CH3C\u2261CH", "isCorrect": true}, {"label": "D", "text": "CH2=CH2", "isCorrect": false}]$q$::jsonb, $q$C$q$, $q$Molar mass = 2 x VD = 40g/mol. Carbon: 36/12=3 atoms. Remaining mass 4g = 4 H atoms. Formula C3H4 = Propyne (CH3C≡CH).$q$, $q$live$q$, $q$A: This is Propane (C3H8). Its molar mass would be 44, which doesn't match our calculated 40. B: This is Propene (C3H6). Its molar mass is 42, which is close but not the 40 we need. D: This is Ethene (C2H4). It only has 24g of carbon per mole, not 36g.$q$),
($q$chemistry_0433$q$, $q$Chemistry$q$, $q$Solubility$q$, $q$Solubility Calculations$q$, $q$Answer a real JAMB 2006 question on Solubility Calculations.$q$, $q$[{"conceptId": "9jaxsa", "weight": 1}]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$application$q$, 72, $q$medium$q$, $q$moderate$q$, $q$[{"option": "B", "explanation": "This value doesn't follow from the figures provided; it might be a result of miscalculating the volume conversion.", "misconceptionId": "unit_conversion_error"}, {"option": "C", "explanation": "You might have accidentally doubled the result or made an error in the division step.", "misconceptionId": "arithmetic_slip"}, {"option": "D", "explanation": "This happens if you divide the mass (34.2) by the volume (50) and then shift decimal points, ignoring the molar mass completely.", "misconceptionId": "formula_recall_error"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$jamb_past_question$q$, 2006, $q$JAMB$q$, $q$When 50cm3 of a saturated solution of sugar of molar mass 342g at 40C was evaporated to dryness, 34.2g of dry solid was obtained. The solubility of sugar at 40C is$q$, $q$[{"label": "A", "text": "2.0 mol dm-3", "isCorrect": true}, {"label": "B", "text": "3.5 mol dm-3", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "C", "text": "7.0 mol dm-3", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "D", "text": "10.0 mol dm-3", "isCorrect": false}]$q$::jsonb, $q$A$q$, $q$Moles of sugar = 34.2/342 = 0.1 mol in 50cm3. Scale to 1000cm3: (0.1x1000)/50 = 2.0 mol/dm3.$q$, $q$live$q$, $q$B: This value doesn't follow from the figures provided; it might be a result of miscalculating the volume conversion. C: You might have accidentally doubled the result or made an error in the division step. D: This happens if you divide the mass (34.2) by the volume (50) and then shift decimal points, ignoring the molar mass completely.$q$),
($q$chemistry_0434$q$, $q$Chemistry$q$, $q$Non-Metals & Their Compounds$q$, $q$Sulphur Compounds$q$, $q$Answer a real JAMB 2006 question on Sulphur Compounds.$q$, $q$[{"conceptId": "2eamtt", "weight": 1}]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$application$q$, 57, $q$low$q$, $q$light$q$, $q$[{"option": "A", "explanation": "Purple is the colour of the reactant (KMnO4). If the solution stays purple, it means no reaction occurred or there is excess reactant left.", "misconceptionId": "confused_similar_concepts"}, {"option": "B", "explanation": "KMnO4 is a soluble salt and doesn't form a purple precipitate in this reaction.", "misconceptionId": "misunderstood_terminology"}, {"option": "C", "explanation": "While the solution becomes colourless, no solid (precipitate) is formed here because all the sulphates produced are soluble in water.", "misconceptionId": "memorized_not_understood"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$jamb_past_question$q$, 2006, $q$JAMB$q$, $q$5SO2(g) + 2KMnO4(aq) + 2H2O(l) -> K2SO4 + 2MnSO4(aq) + 2H2SO4(aq). In the reaction above, the products give a$q$, $q$[{"label": "A", "text": "purple solution", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "B", "text": "purple precipitate", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "C", "text": "colourless precipitate", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "D", "text": "colourless solution", "isCorrect": true}]$q$::jsonb, $q$D$q$, $q$KMnO4 is purple due to MnO4-. SO2 reduces Mn from +7 (purple) to +2 (Mn2+, colourless). All products soluble, so result is a clear colourless solution.$q$, $q$live$q$, $q$A: Purple is the colour of the reactant (KMnO4). If the solution stays purple, it means no reaction occurred or there is excess reactant left. B: KMnO4 is a soluble salt and doesn't form a purple precipitate in this reaction. C: While the solution becomes colourless, no solid (precipitate) is formed here because all the sulphates produced are soluble in water.$q$),
($q$chemistry_0435$q$, $q$Chemistry$q$, $q$Organic Chemistry$q$, $q$Chemical Tests for Functional Groups$q$, $q$Answer a real JAMB 2006 question on Chemical Tests for Functional Groups.$q$, $q$[{"conceptId": "lws0hj", "weight": 1}]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$recall$q$, 37, $q$medium$q$, $q$none$q$, $q$[{"option": "A", "explanation": "The hydroxyl group (found in alcohols) is not acidic enough to react with NaHCO3.", "misconceptionId": "missing_prerequisite"}, {"option": "B", "explanation": "Aldehydes (-CHO) do not react with carbonates; they have their own tests like Fehling's or Tollen's.", "misconceptionId": "confused_similar_concepts"}, {"option": "D", "explanation": "The carbonyl group (found in ketones) is neutral toward NaHCO3.", "misconceptionId": "memorized_not_understood"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$jamb_past_question$q$, 2006, $q$JAMB$q$, $q$Which of the following functional groups will give gas bubbles when treated with a saturated solution of sodium hydrogen trioxocarbonate (IV)?$q$, $q$[{"label": "A", "text": "-OH", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "B", "text": "-CHO", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "C", "text": "-COOH", "isCorrect": true}, {"label": "D", "text": ">C=O", "isCorrect": false}]$q$::jsonb, $q$C$q$, $q$Only acids are strong enough to displace CO2 from NaHCO3. The -COOH group (carboxylic acids) reacts with NaHCO3 to give a salt, water, and effervescence of CO2.$q$, $q$live$q$, $q$A: The hydroxyl group (found in alcohols) is not acidic enough to react with NaHCO3. B: Aldehydes (-CHO) do not react with carbonates; they have their own tests like Fehling's or Tollen's. D: The carbonyl group (found in ketones) is neutral toward NaHCO3.$q$),
($q$chemistry_0436$q$, $q$Chemistry$q$, $q$Non-Metals & Their Compounds$q$, $q$Properties of Nitric Acid — Oxidizing Action$q$, $q$Answer a real JAMB 2007 question on Properties of Nitric Acid — Oxidizing Action.$q$, $q$[{"conceptId": "brt8ck", "weight": 1}]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$application$q$, 57, $q$low$q$, $q$light$q$, $q$[{"option": "B", "explanation": "While concentrated nitric acid is indeed volatile (it fumes in air), this has nothing to do with why it doesn't produce hydrogen gas.", "misconceptionId": "confused_similar_concepts"}, {"option": "C", "explanation": "Passivity only happens with specific metals like Iron, Aluminum, or Chromium in concentrated HNO3. It doesn't explain the general behavior of the acid with all metals.", "misconceptionId": "overgeneralized_rule"}, {"option": "D", "explanation": "You're likely thinking of passivity mentioned in option C. Forming an oxide layer is a result of passivity, but the main reason H2 isn't collected is the oxidation of the gas itself.", "misconceptionId": "memorized_not_understood"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$jamb_past_question$q$, 2007, $q$JAMB$q$, $q$Trioxonitrate (V) acid is not used for preparing hydrogen gas from metals because it$q$, $q$[{"label": "A", "text": "is strongly oxidizing", "isCorrect": true}, {"label": "B", "text": "is volatile", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "C", "text": "renders the metal passive", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "D", "text": "forms a layer of oxide on the metal", "isCorrect": false}]$q$::jsonb, $q$A$q$, $q$HNO3 is a powerful oxidizing agent. As soon as hydrogen forms, the acid oxidizes it to water, and the acid is reduced to nitrogen oxides. Result: water and brown fumes, not H2 gas.$q$, $q$live$q$, $q$B: While concentrated nitric acid is indeed volatile (it fumes in air), this has nothing to do with why it doesn't produce hydrogen gas. C: Passivity only happens with specific metals like Iron, Aluminum, or Chromium in concentrated HNO3. It doesn't explain the general behavior of the acid with all metals. D: You're likely thinking of passivity mentioned in option C. Forming an oxide layer is a result of passivity, but the main reason H2 isn't collected is the oxidation of the gas itself.$q$),
($q$chemistry_0437$q$, $q$Chemistry$q$, $q$Non-Metals & Their Compounds$q$, $q$Sulphur Compounds$q$, $q$Answer a real JAMB 2007 question on Sulphur Compounds.$q$, $q$[{"conceptId": "2eamtt", "weight": 1}]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$recall$q$, 25, $q$low$q$, $q$none$q$, $q$[{"option": "A", "explanation": "Nitrogen is mostly inert and used to prevent burning (like in food packaging), so it wouldn't be useful in a match.", "misconceptionId": "guessed_no_reasoning"}, {"option": "B", "explanation": "Aluminum is a metal; while it can burn in powder form, it isn't used in common household matches.", "misconceptionId": "memorized_not_understood"}, {"option": "C", "explanation": "Copper is used for wiring and coins, not as a combustible material for matches.", "misconceptionId": "confused_similar_concepts"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$jamb_past_question$q$, 2007, $q$JAMB$q$, $q$An element used in the production of matches is$q$, $q$[{"label": "A", "text": "nitrogen", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "B", "text": "aluminium", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "C", "text": "copper", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "D", "text": "sulphur", "isCorrect": true}]$q$::jsonb, $q$D$q$, $q$Match heads contain sulphur as a fuel plus oxidizing agents like potassium chlorate. Red phosphorus is on the striking surface, but sulphur is the element from this list in the match industry.$q$, $q$live$q$, $q$A: Nitrogen is mostly inert and used to prevent burning (like in food packaging), so it wouldn't be useful in a match. B: Aluminum is a metal; while it can burn in powder form, it isn't used in common household matches. C: Copper is used for wiring and coins, not as a combustible material for matches.$q$),
($q$chemistry_0438$q$, $q$Chemistry$q$, $q$Electrolysis$q$, $q$Faraday's Laws$q$, $q$Answer a real JAMB 2007 question on Faraday's Laws.$q$, $q$[{"conceptId": "cbvosp", "weight": 1}]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$application$q$, 84, $q$medium$q$, $q$heavy$q$, $q$[{"option": "A", "explanation": "This might happen if you made a slight arithmetic error during the final division or rounded your numbers too early.", "misconceptionId": "arithmetic_slip"}, {"option": "C", "explanation": "This error usually comes from forgetting to account for the 2 electrons needed for Copper (II), essentially treating it as a valency of 1, but then making another mistake in the multiplication.", "misconceptionId": "formula_recall_error"}, {"option": "D", "explanation": "This result comes if you forget to convert your final answer from seconds to minutes, or if you multiplied by 60 instead of dividing.", "misconceptionId": "unit_conversion_error"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$jamb_past_question$q$, 2007, $q$JAMB$q$, $q$How long would it take to deposit 0.08g of copper from CuCl2 solution by passing a current of 0.5A? [Cu = 64, 1F = 96500 C mol-1]$q$, $q$[{"label": "A", "text": "6 mins", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "B", "text": "8 mins", "isCorrect": true}, {"label": "C", "text": "24 mins", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "D", "text": "48 mins", "isCorrect": false}]$q$::jsonb, $q$B$q$, $q$Cu2+ needs z=2. t = mzF/(MI) = (0.08x2x96500)/(64x0.5) = 15440/32 = 482.5s = ~8 minutes.$q$, $q$live$q$, $q$A: This might happen if you made a slight arithmetic error during the final division or rounded your numbers too early. C: This error usually comes from forgetting to account for the 2 electrons needed for Copper (II), essentially treating it as a valency of 1, but then making another mistake in the multiplication. D: This result comes if you forget to convert your final answer from seconds to minutes, or if you multiplied by 60 instead of dividing.$q$),
($q$chemistry_0439$q$, $q$Chemistry$q$, $q$Organic Chemistry$q$, $q$Functional Group Identification — Esters$q$, $q$Answer a real JAMB 2007 question on Functional Group Identification — Esters.$q$, $q$[{"conceptId": "kmmznm", "weight": 1}]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$application$q$, 57, $q$low$q$, $q$light$q$, $q$[{"option": "A", "explanation": "You likely swapped the two sides. Propyl would mean the alcohol side had 3 carbons, and ethanoate would mean the acid side had only 2.", "misconceptionId": "confused_similar_concepts"}, {"option": "B", "explanation": "Butanoate would require 4 carbons on the acid side. You probably counted all the carbons in the entire molecule together instead of splitting them at the oxygen.", "misconceptionId": "misunderstood_terminology"}, {"option": "D", "explanation": "This name suggests 1 carbon on the alcohol side and 4 on the acid side. Always split the molecule at the single bond O to avoid this.", "misconceptionId": "memorized_not_understood"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$jamb_past_question$q$, 2007, $q$JAMB$q$, $q$The IUPAC nomenclature of the compound CH3CH2COOCH2CH3 is$q$, $q$[{"label": "A", "text": "propyl ethanoate", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "B", "text": "ethyl butanoate", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "C", "text": "ethyl propanoate", "isCorrect": true}, {"label": "D", "text": "methyl butanoate", "isCorrect": false}]$q$::jsonb, $q$C$q$, $q$Alcohol side: -CH2CH3 = ethyl. Acid side (3 carbons incl. carbonyl): CH3-CH2-CO- = propanoate. Together: ethyl propanoate.$q$, $q$live$q$, $q$A: You likely swapped the two sides. Propyl would mean the alcohol side had 3 carbons, and ethanoate would mean the acid side had only 2. B: Butanoate would require 4 carbons on the acid side. You probably counted all the carbons in the entire molecule together instead of splitting them at the oxygen. D: This name suggests 1 carbon on the alcohol side and 4 on the acid side. Always split the molecule at the single bond O to avoid this.$q$),
($q$chemistry_0440$q$, $q$Chemistry$q$, $q$Atomic Structure & Bonding$q$, $q$Electronic Configuration and Periodic Blocks$q$, $q$Answer a real JAMB 2007 question on Electronic Configuration and Periodic Blocks.$q$, $q$[{"conceptId": "7u41s5", "weight": 1}]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$recall$q$, 25, $q$low$q$, $q$light$q$, $q$[{"option": "A", "explanation": "You've identified the number of electrons in the outermost shell (the M shell), but the question specifically asks for the L shell.", "misconceptionId": "rushed_under_pressure"}, {"option": "C", "explanation": "18 is the maximum capacity of the M shell (2n^2 where n=3), not the L shell.", "misconceptionId": "formula_recall_error"}, {"option": "D", "explanation": "2 is the number of electrons in the first shell (K shell).", "misconceptionId": "confused_similar_concepts"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$jamb_past_question$q$, 2007, $q$JAMB$q$, $q$How many electrons are in the L shell of 31/15 P?$q$, $q$[{"label": "A", "text": "5", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "B", "text": "8", "isCorrect": true}, {"label": "C", "text": "18", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "D", "text": "2", "isCorrect": false}]$q$::jsonb, $q$B$q$, $q$Atomic number 15. Configuration: 2(K), 8(L), 5(M). L shell has 8 electrons.$q$, $q$live$q$, $q$A: You've identified the number of electrons in the outermost shell (the M shell), but the question specifically asks for the L shell. C: 18 is the maximum capacity of the M shell (2n^2 where n=3), not the L shell. D: 2 is the number of electrons in the first shell (K shell).$q$),
($q$chemistry_0441$q$, $q$Chemistry$q$, $q$Chemical Combination$q$, $q$Molarity and Mass Calculations$q$, $q$Answer a real JAMB 2007 question on Molarity and Mass Calculations.$q$, $q$[{"conceptId": "ucnecz", "weight": 1}]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$application$q$, 72, $q$low$q$, $q$moderate$q$, $q$[{"option": "A", "explanation": "This looks like an arithmetic error, possibly from miscalculating the molar mass or the multiplication.", "misconceptionId": "arithmetic_slip"}, {"option": "C", "explanation": "This result comes from multiplying the molar mass by 1.5 instead of 3. You likely forgot to account for the 2.0 dm3 volume.", "misconceptionId": "rushed_under_pressure"}, {"option": "D", "explanation": "This is simply the molar mass of 1 mole of NaCl. You forgot to multiply by the molarity and the volume!", "misconceptionId": "missing_prerequisite"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$jamb_past_question$q$, 2007, $q$JAMB$q$, $q$What weight of NaCl is needed to make 2.0 dm3 of a 1.5M solution? [Na = 23, Cl = 35.5]$q$, $q$[{"label": "A", "text": "116 g", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "B", "text": "175.5 g", "isCorrect": true}, {"label": "C", "text": "87 g", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "D", "text": "58.5 g", "isCorrect": false}]$q$::jsonb, $q$B$q$, $q$Moles = 1.5 x 2.0 = 3.0 moles. Molar mass NaCl = 58.5g/mol. Mass = 3.0 x 58.5 = 175.5g.$q$, $q$live$q$, $q$A: This looks like an arithmetic error, possibly from miscalculating the molar mass or the multiplication. C: This result comes from multiplying the molar mass by 1.5 instead of 3. You likely forgot to account for the 2.0 dm3 volume. D: This is simply the molar mass of 1 mole of NaCl. You forgot to multiply by the molarity and the volume!$q$);
